WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.055 --> 00:00:02.460
- [Presenter] North Korean
leader, Kim Jong-un,

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.970
has been trying to build
a new image for himself.

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:08.293
He's gotten a new wardrobe
and lost some weight.

00:00:09.198 --> 00:00:11.561
He's also been showing emotion.

00:00:11.561 --> 00:00:14.539
(crowd cheering)

00:00:14.539 --> 00:00:17.210
While still being the Commander-in-Chief,

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:19.460
who oversees epic missile launches.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:20.750
- He's going for versatility.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:22.630
North Korea's propaganda strategy

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:27.630
under Kim Jong-un are
modernization and transparency.

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:30.120
- [Presenter] The shift
comes as the regime

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.410
is facing a lot of challenges at home.

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:35.630
North Korean analysts say
adapting the regime's propaganda

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:38.291
is one way to keep the
message under control.

00:00:38.291 --> 00:00:42.210
So we examine recent footage
that shows Kim's efforts

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.350
to modernize what his predecessors did,

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:46.320
as he redefines what it
means to be the leader

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.783
of one of the world's
most isolated regimes.

00:00:50.771 --> 00:00:53.270
Kim Jong-un is the third
leader of North Korea

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:55.133
after his father and grandfather.

00:00:55.133 --> 00:00:57.875
He took power in late 2011

00:00:57.875 --> 00:01:00.900
when he was believed to
be just 27 years old.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:02.340
- [Rachel] Kim Jong-un started off

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:05.590
building his public image
modeled on his grandfather.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:07.820
- [Presenter] Rachel Lee is
a former North Korea analyst

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:10.410
for the US government who
now works for a website

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:12.757
that provides analysis about the regime.

00:01:12.757 --> 00:01:16.070
Until recently, Kim
Jong-un had been borrowing

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:18.382
from his grandfather's
propaganda playbook.

00:01:18.382 --> 00:01:21.354
- [Rachel] The way he likes
to give public speeches.

00:01:21.354 --> 00:01:23.830
When you see, Kim Jong-un going out

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:26.560
visiting places or giving inspections,

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.510
you see him mingling with people.

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:31.520
So when people see him, they're
reminded of Kim Il-sung,

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.700
but in recent years, he's
broken out of that shell

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:36.260
now and he's trying to
build his own brand.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:37.190
- [Presenter] And this rebranding

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:39.890
comes amid the country's
mounting economic problems,

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:41.760
as a result of ongoing sanctions

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.310
and the pandemic lockdown.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:45.660
Plus, there's been major
flooding and crop failures

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.296
that have led to food shortages.

00:01:47.296 --> 00:01:49.500
A state run South Korean think tank

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:50.970
estimates that the north needs more

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:53.310
than 5 million tons of food annually.

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:55.360
The last year it could
have faced food shortages

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.430
of more than 1 million tons.

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:59.620
That food deficit means
an average North Korean

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:02.260
is getting around 450 calories fewer

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:04.840
than what's recommended
by the United Nations.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.950
Lee says citizens won't
believe the regime,

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:08.750
if all these issues are covered up.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:10.670
- So, North Korea has
really made an effort

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:14.060
to be more transparent, to
be persuasive to the people,

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:16.360
and also to make the
content more interesting.

00:02:17.412 --> 00:02:19.520
- [Presenter] To do that,
Kim's been attempting

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:22.340
to break the mold by trying new things.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.941
Like in this highly edited
video that showcases the launch

00:02:25.941 --> 00:02:28.820
of what Pyongyang described
as a next generation

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:30.823
intercontinental ballistic missile.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:35.210
- [Rachel] Him walking
out in front of a missile,

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:37.040
you know, that's something
that you would see maybe

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:38.980
in a Mission Impossible movie.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:40.200
- [Presenter] This is a big departure

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.850
from previous weapons tests,

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:44.179
where Kim often watched
from inside a control room.

00:02:44.179 --> 00:02:47.010
And the event was announced

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.702
by a news anchor describing what happened.

00:02:49.702 --> 00:02:53.350
Lee says this kind of elaborate production

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:55.410
can drum up support for the military,

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.851
even amid questions about the
regime's weapons capability.

00:02:58.851 --> 00:03:01.160
About a week after the launch,

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:03.660
South Korean officials
questioned whether the ICBM

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.810
was really new, saying it appeared to be

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:08.253
just a dressed up
version of a predecessor.

00:03:10.887 --> 00:03:14.080
And this stylized video
could also have been produced

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.940
to mollify the North Korean
public about a missile test

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:20.400
in mid-March that South Korea's
military said had failed.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.540
- They went out all the way

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:24.620
to make the video a lot more special

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:26.190
to kind of bending over backwards, right?

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:28.200
Overcompensating for what happened.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.720
But if they continue to do this,

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.003
then maybe this is the
beginning of a new trend.

00:03:33.003 --> 00:03:35.240
- Pyongyang hasn't commented on the test

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:37.637
or the ICBM launch skepticism.

00:03:37.637 --> 00:03:40.550
What's also changing is the clothing.

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:42.880
And Kim's various outfits
are a stark contrast

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:44.690
to his father and grandfather.

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:46.420
They normally stuck to a uniform,

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:48.890
whether it was a suit
and neck tie or Mao suits

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:52.225
which were popularized by
communist China's first leader.

00:03:52.225 --> 00:03:56.300
Kim has also allowed t-shirts
that North Korea experts say

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:58.510
appeared to have his
face emblazoned on it.

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:00.270
Like at this concert last fall.

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:02.730
- It's something that you
see all the time in the west,

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:05.702
and maybe it was somebody's
idea to become more modernized.

00:04:05.702 --> 00:04:07.370
- [Presenter] Lee says this type

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:09.600
of casual iconography
would never have happened

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.310
during the regime under
Kim's father and grandfather,

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:14.760
because propaganda has
always portrayed the leaders

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:17.330
as sacred and powerful godlike figures.

00:04:17.330 --> 00:04:20.260
So this modernizing of
content marks a turning point,

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:21.840
and could come from a desire for Kim

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:23.510
to reach a younger generation.

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:24.660
- [Rachel] They're more knowledgeable

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:27.130
about the outside world, a
huge portion of the population

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:30.640
that has been exposed
to dramas and movies,

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:33.100
hence the need to modernize content.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:35.690
It has to evolve so that
people keep tuning in

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:37.350
to national television.

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:39.340
- [Presenter] Another part
of Kim's propaganda change

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:43.010
more transparency, especially
about the food shortage.

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:44.760
This was also something that Kim's father

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.630
had to manage during his tenure.

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:48.740
At the time, there was
no real acknowledgement

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:51.560
of its severity, even though
as many as 1 million people

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:53.290
in North Korea had died.

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:55.700
- North Korean state media used terms

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:58.450
like food shortages and food problem.

00:04:58.450 --> 00:05:01.690
You would see a more watered down account

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:03.200
of what was going on.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:05.280
- [Presenter] Eventually
the US and United Nations

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:06.960
stepped in to provide food aid

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:10.088
and helped to bring the famine
to an end by the late 1990s.

00:05:10.088 --> 00:05:12.310
But fast forward some 20 years,

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:14.340
and Kim Jong-un is going
about it differently

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:16.490
by openly talking about the food situation

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:18.053
that the country faces today.

00:05:19.266 --> 00:05:23.550
This documentary called the
Great Year of Victory in 2021

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:25.532
aired on state media in February.

00:05:25.532 --> 00:05:28.440
The narrator says the phrase food crisis,

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.170
something that North Korea analysts say

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:33.250
has never been uttered before
in the country's propaganda.

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:36.417
(man speaking Korean)

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:46.950
While Kim has said, the
country is investing

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:49.930
in agriculture and making
progress on the food shortage,

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:51.770
he's also been physically showing citizens

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:53.603
that he understands their struggles.

00:05:54.729 --> 00:05:57.960
Last June, he appeared at a party meeting

00:05:57.960 --> 00:05:59.688
looking considerably thinner,

00:05:59.688 --> 00:06:01.290
and news of his weight loss

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:03.373
was widely reported on state media.

00:06:06.732 --> 00:06:08.790
- [Rachel] And also, you see Jim Jong-un

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:10.560
giving a speech and he was in tears,

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:12.460
and he was choking up as he talked

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:14.470
about the hardships that
people had to go through.

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:17.637
(Kim speaking Korean)

00:06:21.778 --> 00:06:24.380
- [Presenter] North Korea
analysts say, it's hard to tell

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:26.730
if this new propaganda style is working,

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:28.470
and whether younger people are relating

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:30.610
to it more than earlier forms.

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:32.500
Ultimately, propaganda is not about

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:34.700
getting anyone's approval, but shaping

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:37.430
and controlling people's
perception of the leadership,

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:39.370
especially during challenging times

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:41.863
as Kim enters the second
decade of his rule.

